🛠️ Phase 1: Extension Skeleton & DuckDB Interop
The first goal is simply to get DuckDB to recognize the Rust extension and successfully extract data from a SQL query.

Cargo Setup: Define the project as a cdylib (C dynamic library) so DuckDB can load it at runtime. Add duckdb-rs with the buildtime_bindgen and extension features.
The Entrypoint: Implement the #[duckdb_entrypoint] macro in lib.rs to register a custom Table Function or COPY handler (e.g., COPY ... TO 'out.pmtiles' (FORMAT ARROWTILES)).
Data Extraction: Write the function that accepts DuckDB's internal DataChunks and simply prints the row count to the console.
IMPORTANT

User Review Required: Do you want the SQL API to act as a custom COPY function (COPY my_table TO 'out.pmtiles' (FORMAT ARROWTILES)), or as a Table Function (SELECT build_arrowtiles('my_table', 'out.pmtiles'))? A custom COPY function is the idiomatic DuckDB way.

🧮 Phase 2: Arrow Serialization
Once we have access to the DuckDB DataChunks, we need to convert them into Arrow IPC byte buffers.

DuckDB to Arrow: Use the arrow crate to convert the DuckDB DataChunk vectors into an Arrow RecordBatch.
IPC Writer: Use arrow_ipc::writer::FileWriter to serialize the RecordBatch into an in-memory byte vector (Vec<u8>). This byte vector is exactly what a .feather file looks like.
🗺️ Phase 3: Spatial Binning & PMTiles Packaging
This is where the magic happens. We integrate pmtiles-rs to package the Arrow buffers.

Spatial Binning: Calculate the overall spatial bounds of the dataset. For each row, calculate its Quadtree Z/X/Y coordinate, and convert that to a PMTiles Hilbert TileId using the fast_hilbert crate.
PMTiles Writer: Instantiate PmTilesWriter::new(TileType::Unknown).
Write Loop: For each spatial bin (tile), run the Phase 2 Arrow serialization, and pass the resulting Vec<u8> to pmtiles_writer.add_raw_tile(tile_id, byte_buffer).
Finalize: Call finalize() on the PMTiles writer to flush the Root Directory and header to disk.
